---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import Scroll from "../components/Scroll.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { OWN, INHERITED, nOwn } from "../lib/errors.js";


---
<Base
  title="What This Archive Got Wrong"
  eyebrow="Its own errors, gathered"
  dek={`${nOwn} mistakes this archive made and then corrected in public, with what caused each one. An archive that asks to be trusted about other people's errors has to be legible about its own.`}>

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Every page here carries a confidence label, and the method only means anything if
    it is applied to the archive itself. These are errors <strong>this site published</strong> — not
    inherited from the family tree, but made here — together with what caused each one and what it
    should teach the next person.</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>None of these was quietly edited away.</strong> Each correction still stands on the page
      where the error was made, which is why the links below go to the page rather than to an
      apology. Two were caught within hours. One stood for a day — and it was the worst kind, a
      confident correction aimed at the family about their own family.
    </Note>
  </section>

  {OWN.map((e) => (
    <Blk label={e.when} title={e.truth}>
      <Scroll>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td style="white-space:nowrap"><strong>It said</strong></td><td>{e.what}</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="white-space:nowrap"><strong>It is</strong></td><td>{e.truth}</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="white-space:nowrap"><strong>How</strong></td><td>{e.why}</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </Scroll>
      <Note tight>
        <strong>The lesson.</strong> {e.lesson} <a href={u(e.href)}>Where it was corrected →</a>
      </Note>
    </Blk>
  ))}

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And the errors that came with the tree</h2>
    <p class="dek">These are a different thing, and worth keeping separate: claims inherited from the
    exported family tree and overturned here. They are somebody else's mistakes, made in good faith,
    and every one of them is still shown on the page that corrects it rather than deleted.</p>
    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>The tree said</th><th>The record says</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {INHERITED.map(([a, b, href]) => (
            <tr><td>{a}</td><td>{b} <a href={u(href)}>→</a></td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Why this page exists</h2>
    <p class="dek">Because the alternative is worse. An archive that corrects itself silently is
    asking to be trusted on exactly the point it has just proved it can be wrong about — and because
    the pattern in the eight above is more useful than any one of them. Five of the seven are the same
    failure: <strong>a number or a claim taken from a secondary source, or from a bad scan, and not
    checked against the record itself.</strong></p>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.9rem">If you find a ninth, it belongs here.
    <a href={u("/method/")}>How claims are labelled →</a> ·
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>What is still open →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
